<%#  The logo at the top of a transactional email, for THIS shop.

     ── the bug this exists for ───────────────────────────────────────────────

     Every one of these templates read the logo out of a locale key:

       Spree.t(:shop_logo, scope: [:user_emails, :common])

     One shop has a locale file of its own. The other four fall through to the
     default `en`, which belongs to a different brand, so their order
     confirmations, cancellations, shipping notices and payment links all went
     out carrying another company's logo. Measured on the rendered mail:

       ca       cdn.<this brand>/ca/email/..._logo_tm.png
       nz       cdn.<other brand>/email/...bizemail.png
       au       cdn.<other brand>/email/...bizemail.png
       us       cdn.<other brand>/email/...bizemail.png
       nolocal  cdn.<other brand>/email/...bizemail.png

     Nothing failed. The emails built, sent and looked finished.

     ── the first fix, which has since been turned around ─────────────────────

     The store's own mailer_logo attachment was made the primary source, because
     an attachment cannot be another shop's by construction. That held until the
     attachments went stale: see the second comment below, above the branches,
     for where the logo comes from today and why the order changed.

     Active Storage needs a host to build that url, which comes from RAILS_HOST.
     Unset, it raises rather than returning nil, so the rescue is load bearing.
%>
<%
  store = @current_store || Spree::Store.default
  attached = store.mailer_logo.attached? ? store.mailer_logo : (store.logo.attached? ? store.logo : nil)
  link = Spree.t(:shop_url_full, scope: [:user_emails, :common], default: "").presence ||
         "https://#{store.url}"
  fallback = Spree.t(:shop_logo, scope: [:user_emails, :common], default: "").presence
%>
<%#  ── the order these are tried in, and why it was turned around ─────────────

     The attachment used to come first. That was right when it was written: four
     shops had no locale file, so the key fell through to the shared `en` and
     their mail carried another brand's logo. An attachment cannot be another
     shop's, so it was the safer of the two.

     Every shop now has its own locale file, and each one names its own image on
     the client's CDN. Measured on 31 Aug, all seven answer 200:

       every one of the seven names its own file on his CDN, one per shop

     The attachments had not kept up. THREE shops shared one uploaded file
     between them, and a fourth carried the generic one. The shop owner found it
     in a real order confirmation, said the template was calling the old logo,
     and gave the address it should use, which is exactly what his locale file
     already said.

     So his own per-shop URL is preferred now, and the attachment is the fallback
     rather than the winner.

     This is only safe while every shop HAS its own locale file. That is not an
     assumption, it is a gate: script/check_shop_locales fails the build for a
     shop with no wording of its own, and it exists because of the very bug this
     comment opens with. %>
<a href="<%= link %>" target="_blank">
  <% if fallback %>
    <img src="<%= fallback %>" alt="<%= store.name %>" style="display:block;height:54px;max-width:100%;border:0;" width="239">
  <% elsif attached %>
    <%#  Spree's own partial, not url_for.
         url_for(attachment) raised in this context and the rescue turned that
         into a silent fall back to the other brand's logo, which is the exact
         bug this file exists to stop. This is the code path Spree already uses
         for the mailer header, so it builds the url the way the rest of the
         platform does, cdn host and image variant included. %>
    <%= render 'spree/shared/mailer_logo', logo: attached, height: 54 %>
  <% else %>
    <span style="font-size:18px;font-weight:bold;"><%= store.name %></span>
  <% end %>
</a>
